  origins: `**Moralizing self-control** has deep roots. Plato's *Phaedrus* chariot and Aristotle's distinction between the *enkratic* (self-controlled by effort) and the *temperate* (not tempted) person; Victorian character ideals (Smiles, *Self-Help*, 1859); and the American bootstraps ethos.

**Social psychology of strategy use.** Recent experiments show observers penalize people who rely on external aids. Scott & Landy's work on medication and moral character; Kristal & Zlatev (2024) on commitment devices; Gennara, Peetz & Milyavskaya on "strategy beliefs." Duckworth's lab adds "situation neglect" (in preparation).

**Ecological psychology.** Roger Barker (with Herbert Wright and Louise Barker) founded the Midwest Psychological Field Station in 1947. Classic outputs: *One Boy's Day* (1951), *Midwest and Its Children* (1955), *Big School, Small School* (1964), *Ecological Psychology* (1968). Val Curtis (London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine, d. 2021) built "Behaviour Centred Design" on Barker's settings.`,
  evidence: [
    { cite: "Scott & Landy (2023), \"'Good people don't need medication': How moral character beliefs affect medical decision making,\" *OBHDP*", note: "Experiments showing medications are judged inferior to behavior change for the same condition, mediated by beliefs that needing medication signals poor character." },
    { cite: "Kristal & Zlatev (2024), \"Going beyond the 'self' in self-control: Interpersonal consequences of commitment strategies,\" *JPSP*", note: "People who use commitment devices are seen as less trustworthy. That is a real social cost, which may explain underuse." },
    { cite: "Aagaard (2021), students' anti-distraction strategies, *Learning, Media and Technology*", note: "Qualitative interviews: students' blocking strategies work but feel \"embarrassing.\"" },
    { cite: "Gennara, Peetz & Milyavskaya (2023), \"When more is less: Self-control strategies are seen as less indicative of self-control than just willpower,\" *JESP*", note: "Source of the Strategy Beliefs Scale items." },
    { cite: "Barker (1968), *Ecological Psychology*; Barker (1979), \"Settings of a professional lifetime,\" *JPSP*", note: "The theory of behavior settings and Barker's own account of the field station." },
    { cite: "Gautam et al. (2017), \"Trial of a novel intervention to improve multiple food hygiene behaviors in Nepal,\" *AJTMH*", note: "Cluster-randomized trial of kitchen makeovers and pledges. The 1% → 43% figure is for mothers practicing all five behaviors. A large effect from setting redesign plus social commitment." },
    { cite: "Carroll (2023), \"What obesity drugs and antidepressants have in common,\" *New York Times*", note: "First-person essay; the chapter's opening case." },
  ],
  debates: `**Is medication really \"situational\"?** Duckworth's reclassification is philosophically interesting. A drug changes your physiology, which is arguably *you*, not your surroundings. One could say it changes Box Two's inputs from inside the body. The underlying point stands either way: a deliberate, upstream change that reduces the need for in-the-moment resistance shouldn't be stigmatized. Bioethicists debate the related "enhancement vs. treatment" distinction and whether "authentic" achievement requires unaided effort (Sandel, *The Case Against Perfection*, 2007, argues for an ethic of "giftedness").

**Is moralizing willpower irrational?** Some philosophers defend admiring effort. Effortful resistance can express commitment, and relying on external constraints can erode the capacity to self-govern. Others (Gendler; Kennett & Smith) argue the wise agent arranges her circumstances. Aristotle ranked the temperate person, who isn't tempted, above the enkratic person, who resists.

**Barker's legacy.** Barker's methods were labor-intensive and his theory under-specified individual differences within settings. Its modern revival comes through smartphone sensing, place-based public health, and the built-environment research of Ch. 9.

**GLP-1 debates.** Long-term safety, regain after discontinuation, cost and access are open. Unequal access means "situational agency" through medication is unevenly distributed.`,
